27. What if the provider doesn't support idempotency?
This is where things get harder.
Suppose provider has:
POST /charge
but no idempotency key.
You send:
₹1000
and timeout.
Now you don't know whether it charged.
You can't safely send another charge.
You need another mechanism, such as:
GET /transaction/{merchantReference}
or:
query by merchant transaction ID
or:
provider reconciliation file
or another provider-supported lookup mechanism.
If there is no way to determine the outcome, then the system cannot mathematically guarantee safe retry of that external side effect.
This is an important architectural constraint.
________________________________________
28. The "impossible" boundary
Suppose an external service gives you:
POST /doSomething
and nothing else.
No:
idempotency key
query API
transaction ID
reconciliation
You send the request.
You get a timeout.
There are two possible realities:
Reality A:
request never arrived
and:
Reality B:
request arrived and succeeded
Your system observes exactly the same thing:
TIMEOUT
No algorithm inside your service can distinguish those realities.
Therefore:
You cannot guarantee exactly-once business effect against an external system that provides neither idempotency nor outcome discovery.
This is a fundamental distributed-systems limitation, not a Java problem.
